Eadu is like the e- version of communication between Odoo instances.

Right now, people have to open mails, go to the portal of the other Odoo instance,
while they should be able to see all communication from their partners that
also use Odoo in their own instance.

This is still Proof of Concept / Research phase. (the name is just for fun)

To run the 2 instances, you can do: (if eadu is placed next to the odoo repo)

./odoo-bin --addons="../eadu,addons" -d eadu -i eadu

./odoo-bin --addons="../eadu,addons" -d jedha -i eadu -p 8070


Three-instance demo environment
-------------------------------

When this repository is placed next to the Odoo repository, the helper scripts
can create and connect all three EADU demo instances:

```
./eadu_install.sh eadutest
```

The prefix is used to create the following databases and start them on local
ports:

- ``eadutest_eadu`` at http://127.0.0.1:8069
- ``eadutest_ghorman`` at http://127.0.0.1:8070
- ``eadutest_deathstar`` at http://127.0.0.1:8071

``eadu_install.sh`` installs the corresponding demo module in each database,
starts the three servers, configures their local URLs, and creates pairwise EADU
connections. Each database is therefore connected to both of the other demo
databases. The script requires local PostgreSQL access through ``psql`` and
does not overwrite existing databases; use a new prefix for a fresh setup.

To start an already installed set of databases again, run:

```
./eadu_run.sh eadutest
```

Both scripts expect Odoo to be in ``../odoo``. Set ``ODOO_DIR`` to the Odoo
checkout path when it is elsewhere. Installation logs, runtime logs, and PID
files are stored in ``.eadu_instances/<prefix>/``.


Goal
----

The goal of Eadu is to check what the advantages are of synchronizing data between
different Odoo instances instead of using the portal, mails or external systems.


Connecting two instances
------------------------

When you want to connect to another Odoo instance, you do it through its partner form.
You let it generate a token and you pass that token to someone in the other instance.
He will have a partner form of you and he can enter the token.
(this flow can still be improved upon)

Automatically the two instances communicate:

- A portal user is created on each side.
- The partner linked to that user is a child contact of the original partner and it also
  holds the credentials of the portal user in the other instance.
- Those Eadu portal users are portal users with extended portal-user rights.
- If there is no child contact yet for the users that exchanged the tokens, a child
  contact is created for them as well.
- This way, those two can already talk to each other in Discuss.


Architecture
------------

There is one central model: **eadu.partner.any** with the fields
``partner_id`` (eadu partner), ``res_model``, ``res_id``, and ``eadu_ident``.
It expresses: "this model/id in my database corresponds to that id (eadu_ident)
in the database of the eadu partner (the other Odoo instance)."

It is also used for security: an Eadu portal user gets extra access rights based on the
models that have been shared.
(Open question: should we also grant write access here?)

A ``pending_calls`` JSON field on the same model handles the case where calls cannot be
delivered immediately. The calls are stored and replayed when the cron runs or the
connection is restored.


The three-body problem
----------------------

What happens when a Discuss channel involves partners from multiple Odoo instances
(multiple Eadu partners)?

The solution is to acknowledge the original creator of the channel as the *master*
(tracked by a ``master_status`` field on ``eadu.partner.any``).
Messages flow through the master, which then fans them out to all other instances.


Rich-text links
---------------

In the chatter you can link to any object in a message — a product, an attachment,
another partner, etc.
When such a message is sent through a channel and arrives in another Odoo instance,
the link is rewritten to contain the correct id in that instance.
That way the link resolves correctly regardless of which database is viewing it.


Testing strategy
----------------

Because the tests run inside a single Odoo database, actual HTTP calls between
instances are not possible.  Instead, ``res.partner._eadu_call`` is patched with
a mock that intercepts every outgoing RPC and routes it locally.

The mock resolves the portal API key stored on the calling Eadu contact to the
corresponding ``res.users`` record in the same database, then dispatches the call
under that user's access rights.  This exercises the full application code path
— access control, ``eadu.partner.any`` lookups, queue logic — without any network
dependency.

Each "remote instance" in the tests is represented by a separate ``res.company``
inside the same database.  When the top-level partner that holds the Eadu contact
has a ``company_id`` set, records created through the Eadu flow (contacts, users,
mappings) are scoped to that company, making the two companies behave like two
independent Odoo instances.

This trick works well for models that carry a ``company_id`` field, such as
``product.product``.  It does not work for ``discuss.channel``, which has no
``company_id``, so channel mirroring in the tests produces a second channel record
in the same database rather than in a genuinely separate company scope.  The
functional behaviour is still correctly tested; the separation is just logical
rather than company-scoped for that model.

To simulate a connection failure, a test adds the Eadu contact's id to a
``_blocked_partners`` set before the relevant call.  The mock then raises
``EaduConnectionError``, triggering the queue path.  After the block is removed,
``_retry_all_queued_calls()`` is called to verify that queued messages are
delivered in the correct order and the queue is cleared.

To run the tests:

./odoo-bin --addons="../eadu,addons" -d eadu --test-tags eadu -u eadu after installation



